<title>USA: Texaco CEO hopes co can become &quot;model&quot; workplace.</title>
<headline>Texaco CEO hopes co can become &quot;model&quot; workplace.</headline>
<dateline>WHITE PLAINS, N.Y. 1996-11-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Texaco Inc Chairman and Chief Executive Peter Bijur said on Friday he hoped Texaco could &quot;move forward&quot; to become a &quot;model of workplace opportunity for all men and women.&quot;</p>
<p>In a letter to company employees announcing the settlement of a racial discrimination suit filed against in 1994 by black employees, Bijur said Texaco would increase the amount of business it does with minority companies. This would include advertising, legal, banking, investment management and accounting firms.</p>
<p>He also said in the statement that &quot;we will actively work to increase the number of Texaco branded stations owned by minorities and women.</p>
<p>&quot;I have committed myself -- and the entire management team of this company -- to the elimination of any trace of discrimination in Texaco,&quot; he added.</p>
</text>
